# Coco Tree Service — Architecture Rules

## Project Structure

The project follows this structure:

app/
components/
animations/
data/
lib/
public/
types/

## App

The pp/ directory contains Next.js application routes and global application configuration.

Keep pp/page.tsx focused on composing the landing page sections.

Do not place large component implementations directly inside page.tsx.

The main page should orchestrate sections such as:

- Navbar
- Hero
- Introduction
- Services
- Process
- Gallery
- FAQ
- CTA
- Footer

## Components

The components/ directory contains reusable React components.

Organize components by purpose:

components/
+-- ui/
+-- navigation/
+-- sections/
+-- cards/
+-- forms/

### UI

Small reusable interface elements:

- Button
- Icon
- Badge
- Divider

### Navigation

Navigation-related components:

- Navbar
- NavLink
- MobileMenu

### Sections

Major landing page sections:

- Hero
- Introduction
- Services
- Process
- Gallery
- FAQ
- CTA
- Footer

### Cards

Reusable content patterns:

- ServiceCard
- ProcessStep
- GalleryItem
- Testimonial

### Forms

Reusable form elements:

- EstimateForm
- FormField

## Data

The data/ directory contains structured content used by components.

Examples:

data/
+-- services.ts
+-- process.ts
+-- gallery.ts
+-- testimonials.ts
+-- faq.ts

Do not hardcode repeated content directly inside reusable components when it can reasonably be represented as structured data.

## Animations

The nimations/ directory contains reusable animation logic.

Examples:

animations/
+-- heroAnimations.ts
+-- scrollAnimations.ts
+-- parallax.ts
+-- reveal.ts

Do not place large GSAP timelines directly inside unrelated UI components when the animation can be isolated into the animation layer.

Animation logic may remain inside a component when it is small and tightly coupled to that component.

## Lib

The lib/ directory contains shared utilities and application helpers.

Examples:

lib/
+-- utils.ts
+-- validation.ts
+-- constants.ts

Do not place UI components inside lib/.

## Types

The 	ypes/ directory contains shared TypeScript types and interfaces.

Use shared types when multiple components or data modules depend on the same structure.

## Public Assets

Organize public assets by purpose:

public/
+-- images/
    +-- hero/
    +-- services/
    +-- gallery/
    +-- process/
+-- videos/
    +-- hero/
+-- icons/
+-- logo/

Use descriptive filenames.

Avoid generic names such as:

- image1
- image2
- final
- final2
- new-image

## Component Responsibility

Each component should have a clear responsibility.

Prefer:

Section
-> reusable component
-> UI component

over large components containing unrelated responsibilities.

## Reusability

Before creating a new component:

1. Search the existing component library.
2. Determine whether an existing component can be reused.
3. Extend the existing component if appropriate.
4. Create a new component only when the behavior or visual responsibility is meaningfully different.

## Dependencies

Do not introduce dependencies to solve problems that can reasonably be solved with existing project capabilities.

Before installing a package:

1. Check whether the required functionality already exists.
2. Check whether the project already has a suitable dependency.
3. Evaluate bundle size and maintenance impact.
4. Only then introduce the dependency.

## Modification Scope

When implementing a feature, modify only the files required for that feature.

Do not refactor unrelated areas unless explicitly requested or required to prevent a real technical problem.

## Page Composition

The landing page should remain readable at a high level.

Conceptually:

<Navbar />

<main>
  <Hero />
  <Introduction />
  <Services />
  <Process />
  <Gallery />
  <FAQ />
  <CTA />
</main>

<Footer />

The actual implementation may differ when technically necessary, but the page composition should remain easy to understand.

## Server and Client Components

Prefer React Server Components by default.

Use "use client" only when client-side behavior is required, such as:

- state
- browser APIs
- interactive UI
- GSAP
- ScrollTrigger
- Lenis

Do not convert entire sections to Client Components unnecessarily.

## Architecture Principle

The project should remain understandable to a developer who did not create it.

Favor:

- clear naming
- predictable locations
- small responsibilities
- reusable components
- explicit data structures
- minimal dependencies
- minimal duplication
